# The build context is the repository root (`buildctl --local context=.`), so everything below is
# uploaded to the builder unless it is named here.

# A host-built dependency tree is the wrong toolchain's, and it is the single largest thing in the
# tree. The image build runs its own `npm ci`.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# The image build runs its own `npm run build`. A host-built dist/ in the context would be bytes
# nobody gated, sitting where the build is about to write.
dist
.angular

# THE ONE ENTRY THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY. npm ranks a PROJECT .npmrc above ~/.npmrc,
# and the Dockerfile writes ~/.npmrc from the two build-args the release step hands the builder.
# Copied into the context, the committed file — which names the edge vhosts a developer uses, and
# which the builder resolves not at all — would win and break every install. app.yml's Dockerfile
# contract names this exact hazard and offers exactly two ways out; this is one of them.
.npmrc

.git
.gitignore
.vscode
README.md
